# Example Caddy snippets for Matrix + MAS

Adapt hostnames. Point to the binds in `.env` (`127.0.0.1:8008` / `127.0.0.1:8081` by default).

On crew, the live Caddyfile uses explicit `http://` / `https://` site blocks because
`auto_https disable_redirects` is set (HTTP webapp must stay HTTP). Mirror that style.
Also `admin off` means `systemctl reload caddy` cannot reach `:2019` — use
`systemctl restart caddy` after edits.

## Routing rules (MSC4108 QR device linking)

| Host | Path | Backend |
|---|---|---|
| `auth.<guild>` | `/` (incl. `/device`, `/link`, `/authorize`, `/upstream/…`) | **MAS** (`127.0.0.1:8081`) |
| `matrix.<guild>` | `/_synapse/client/rendezvous`, unstable MSC4108 rendezvous | **Synapse** (do not send to MAS) |
| `matrix.<guild>` | `/_matrix/…`, `/.well-known/matrix/…` | **Synapse** |

When `auth` and `matrix` are separate hostnames (structs-tel default), a full reverse_proxy of each host to the right service is enough. If you ever put MAS and Synapse on one hostname, carve MAS paths explicitly and leave rendezvous on Synapse.

```caddy
http://matrix.crew.oh.energy {
	encode zstd gzip
	handle /.well-known/matrix/client {
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *
		header Content-Type application/json
		respond `{"m.homeserver":{"base_url":"https://matrix.crew.oh.energy"},"org.matrix.msc2965.authentication":{"issuer":"https://auth.crew.oh.energy/","account":"https://auth.crew.oh.energy/account"}}`
	}
	handle /.well-known/matrix/server {
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *
		header Content-Type application/json
		respond `{"m.server":"matrix.crew.oh.energy:443"}`
	}
	# Synapse: CS API, federation via 443, MSC4108 rendezvous
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8008
	}
}

https://matrix.crew.oh.energy {
	encode zstd gzip
	handle /.well-known/matrix/client {
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *
		header Content-Type application/json
		respond `{"m.homeserver":{"base_url":"https://matrix.crew.oh.energy"},"org.matrix.msc2965.authentication":{"issuer":"https://auth.crew.oh.energy/","account":"https://auth.crew.oh.energy/account"}}`
	}
	handle /.well-known/matrix/server {
		header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *
		header Content-Type application/json
		respond `{"m.server":"matrix.crew.oh.energy:443"}`
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8008
	}
}

# MAS: OIDC + human UI including /device and /link (MSC4108 confirmation pages)
http://auth.crew.oh.energy {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}

https://auth.crew.oh.energy {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}
```

If `MATRIX_SERVER_NAME` is a bare domain that differs from the client API host, put `/.well-known/matrix/*` on the **server_name** host per Matrix delegation rules.
